      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2953.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 743,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the most abundant and essential substance on Earth, uniquely suited to support life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, based on its states, properties, and importance:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and use for rain (found in oceans, rivers, and lakes).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F)—found in glaciers, ice caps, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils or evaporates into the air—found in humidity, clouds, and steam.\n\n### 2. Unique and Miraculous Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a delivery system, carrying vital chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes (through our bloodstreams, through plant roots, etc.).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float. In nature, this means lakes freeze from the top down, allowing fish and marine life to survive underneath the ice during winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why oceans help regulate the temperature of the entire planet, and why our bodies don't overheat instantly.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing water bugs to walk on it) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Earth is the \"Blue Planet\":** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite how much water there is, about **96.5% of it is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about 2.5% is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\n*   **Biological Necessity:** Life as we know it cannot exist without water. The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. We use it to regulate body temperature, flush out waste, lubricate joints, and digest food. \n*   **Ecosystems:** Water shapes the geography of our planet, carves canyons, drives weather patterns, and provides habitats for millions of plant and animal species.\n\n**In short:** Water is the fundamental fluid of life—a simple molecule with complex, life-sustaining properties that makes our planet unique in the universe."
